#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# kronecker_symbol

#% text
# `kronecker_symbol(a, n)` gives the Kronecker symbol `(a/n)`, the Jacobi symbol extended to all integers `n`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# For odd `n > 0` it is `jacobi_symbol(a, n)`; the factor 2 contributes 0 for even `a` and 1 or -1 by `mod(a, 8)`, and `n = -1` contributes the sign of `a`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
kronecker_symbol(3, 8)
#% output
#= -1

#% cell
kronecker_symbol(5, 8)
#% output
#= -1

#% cell
kronecker_symbol(-1, -5)
#% output
#= -1

#% cell
kronecker_symbol(2, 15)
#% output
#= 1

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# A Dirichlet character modulo 12, the Kronecker symbol of the discriminant 12:

#% cell
table(kronecker_symbol(12, k), k, 12)
#% output
#= [1, 0, 0, 0, -1, 0, -1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# For odd positive `n` it is the Jacobi symbol:

#% cell
[kronecker_symbol(10, 21), jacobi_symbol(10, 21)]
#% output
#= [-1, -1]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `jacobi_symbol`, `mod`
